Inheritance and the class hierarchy.

Inheritance lets one class build on another. A subclass extends a superclass, automatically gaining its fields and methods, and then adds or adjusts whatever it needs. It models an "is-a" relationship — a savings account is a bank account — and it lets shared behaviour live in one place, instead of being copied everywhere.

You express this with the extends keyword. Suppose a bank account has a balance and a deposit method. A savings account extends bank account, so it gets balance and deposit for free, and it adds its own field, a rate, and its own method, add-interest, which uses that inherited balance. One important limit: a single class can extend only one superclass. Java has single inheritance of classes. Interfaces, which we'll meet next, are how you mix in more than one set of behaviours.

Often a subclass needs to build on the parent, rather than replace it, and for that you use super, which refers to the superclass. Super, with arguments, in a constructor, calls the parent's constructor — and it must be the very first statement, because the parent part of the object has to be initialized before the child. If you don't call it explicitly, Java inserts a call to the no-argument super for you. Separately, super dot method calls the parent's version of a method you've overridden, which is useful when you want to extend behaviour, not throw it away. So a savings account constructor calls super with the opening balance first, to initialize the bank account part, and then sets its own rate.

Now, overriding. A subclass can override an inherited method — same signature, new body — to specialize it. You should mark it with the at-Override annotation. It's not strictly required, but it makes the compiler check that you actually are overriding something. Without it, a mistyped method name silently becomes a brand-new method instead of an override, and that's a nasty bug to chase. As an example, a savings account might override deposit to first call super dot deposit, doing the normal deposit, and then log the transaction as its own extra step.

Two edges of the hierarchy are worth knowing. Mark a class final, and it cannot be extended at all — string is final, which is why nobody subclasses it. Mark a method final, and it can't be overridden. And at the very top of every hierarchy sits Object. Walk any chain of extends upward, and you always arrive at Object — which is exactly why every object has equals, hash code, and to string.

One last piece of judgment. Inheritance is powerful, but it's tightly coupling. A subclass depends on its parent's internals, and a change upstream can ripple down. So reach for it when there's a genuine is-a relationship, and real behaviour to share. When you're tempted to inherit just to reuse some code, remember there's usually a looser alternative — composition — which the last section of this module argues you should often prefer.
